A||||5||fierobear||Blue Book prices of Fieros going up?||||1||
Z||000000||fierobear||01-22-2001||01:24 AM||||I have heard more than one person tell me that the Kelly Blue Book price of Fieros has been rising. I haven't seen a Blue Book for a year or more. Does anyone have any good data on this?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||01-22-2001||01:53 AM||||Go to: <A HREF="http://www.kbb.com" TARGET=_blank>www.kbb.com</A> <P>It's the Kelly Blue Book Site.<BR>My car went up $50.00 since last year. But I think this is because of mileage. The less miles than average you have, the value increases slightly. Last year the average listed was in the low 100,000 miles. It is in the 110,000 now. My car is in the low 80,000. I am waiting for the 2001 Price Guide to come out before I judge if my car has increased. With all the work I have done on my car, I had it appraised. Now it is considered a "Classic" (15 years old) and is insured for the appraised value (and it is exempt from emission testing!)||||reg||1
Z||000002||ur2biteme||01-22-2001||03:47 AM||||i've noticed the price has been inching up since '97.  i'm not so sure it has to do with mileage so much as the fact that the car is getting older, the demand is going up, and well, let's face it, the car's price bottomed out quite a while ago.  all cars do this eventually it seems. i'm sure the mileage helps, but when all is said and done, with cars this old, the mileage usually isnt as much a factor as, say, a 5 year old car.  just my opinion, i could be wrong<P>------------------<BR>Bob's '87 econo coupe<BR>170k awaiting a tranny:(<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||01-24-2001||04:02 PM||||I put in my cars and some fake ones and noticed that the GT's are the only ones that seem to have an unrealistic inflated value. So the demand for the GT must be greater.<BR>I did a mileage test<BR>Example:<BR>My 86 SE 2M6 with 60k    $3315<BR>My 86 SE 2M6 with 105K   $3115  (fake)<BR>An 86 GT with 105K       $4690  (fake)<BR>An 86 GT with 60K        $5090  (fake)<BR>All cars have the same options<BR>So half the mileage only gets  you a $200<BR>increase in price for an SE but a $310 increase for a GT<BR>Lastly <BR>My 86 SE 2M4 with 116K $2570<BR>My 86 SE 2M4 with 199K $2370 (fake)<BR>Funny how options like a cd player and leather made NO difference in the price<BR>but do make a heck of a difference in the <BR>comfort of a car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||01-25-2001||03:06 PM||||hoo-ray for GT's!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Mike<BR>1986.5 GT 5 speed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000005||ChadMan||01-25-2001||03:51 PM||||That's really interesting that KBB doesn't assign much difference in value between low-mileage and high-mileage cars.  I mean, 100K Fieros are common (no offense -- I know some high-mileage cars are in great shape), but 60K mile cars are getting harder and harder to find, and seem to be commanding premiums of $500-$1000 over 100K-and-up cars.||||reg||4
